# Copy to .dev.vars for local development. .dev.vars is gitignored — never commit it.
#
# In preview/production these are Worker secrets, set with:
#   wrangler secret put JWT_SECRET --env production
#
# Auxilium runs fully without any of these. Missing keys degrade cleanly:
# auth falls back to a development-only signing key (and logs a loud warning),
# and AI-assisted triage notes simply don't render. Nothing crashes, nothing
# hard-blocks. You can exercise the whole product before adding a single key.

# Signs session cookies. REQUIRED in production — the Worker refuses to issue
# sessions in production if this is unset.
JWT_SECRET="dev-only-change-me"

# Salts the session token derivation, kept separate from JWT_SECRET so rotating
# one does not invalidate the other's guarantees.
SESSION_SECRET="dev-only-change-me-too"

# Optional. Enables AI-drafted triage summaries on the NRI command center.
# Absent → the panel renders a "not configured" note and the rule-based
# explanation stands on its own. NRI scoring itself is never AI-dependent.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""

# ── Stripe ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional. Absent → billing is switched OFF, not broken: the connect and
# invoicing endpoints answer "not configured", the webhook refuses everything,
# and every other part of the product — the contributions ledger, the share
# ratio, scoring, claims — works exactly as normal.
#
# Use test keys locally. `sk_test_...` and a webhook secret from either the
# Stripe CLI (`stripe listen --forward-to localhost:8787/api/stripe/webhook`)
# or the dashboard endpoint you create for the deployed environment.

# Server-side API key. Never prefix with pk_ — that is the publishable key and
# belongs in a browser, not here.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=""

# Signs incoming webhooks. Without it the webhook endpoint refuses every
# request rather than trusting unsigned input — an unsigned webhook that writes
# to the ledger would let anyone fabricate settled contributions.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# Where Stripe returns a ministry after Connect onboarding. Defaults to the
# request origin, which is right for local development and for a single-domain
# deployment. Set it explicitly if the public hostname differs.
STRIPE_RETURN_URL=""

# ── Alerts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional. Absent → alerts are still raised and stored; nobody is emailed, and
# `/api/health` reports that plainly. It has to say so, because the symptom of
# getting this wrong is an inbox that stays quiet — which is exactly what a
# healthy system looks like.
#
# Locally you almost certainly want none of these: with no key, `sendEmail`
# logs the whole message it would have sent, which is easier to read than a
# real inbox and cannot mail a real ministry by accident.

# Resend API key. One POST, no SDK — the same reasoning as the hand-written
# Stripe client.
RESEND_API_KEY=""

# The From address. Must be on a domain verified with the mail provider, or
# every send fails with a 403 that reads like an auth problem.
ALERT_FROM_EMAIL=""

# Where operator alerts go — a ledger that will not reconcile, a monthly close
# that failed. Comma-separated for more than one. Deliberately not sent to
# ministries: a discrepancy is usually a delivery failure on our side, and a
# list of charge ids is alarming and unactionable to them in equal measure.
ALERT_EMAIL=""
